{
 "id": "2021-wu-design",
 "name": "Design Intensity in Urban Green Space: Ranking and Restorativeness",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: the 20 pictures from Figure 1 of Wu, Zhuo, Liu, Yu, Huang & Liu (2021), \"The Relationships between Perceived Design Intensity, Preference, Restorativeness and Eye Movements in Designed Urban Green Space\", International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health 18(20): 10944 (CC BY 4.0). Five urban green space scenes (waterfront plaza, rockery waterfall, lawn, garden path, pond), each edited by the authors in Photoshop into four design intensities by adding or removing elements such as pavilions, sculptures, stones and flowers.\n\nEach participant is randomly assigned to one scene. The four versions appear together in a random 2 × 2 arrangement for 15 seconds, then participants rank them by preference (Law of Comparative Judgment; each picture gets a different rank, 1 = lowest, 4 = highest). Each version is then shown alone in random order and rated on the paper's four-item restorative potential scale: being away, extent, fascination and compatibility (1 = fully disagree, 7 = fully agree). Records are keyed by set letter and figure position (a1 … e4); the page source holds the paper's median preference and restorative ratings per intensity. Analysis as in the paper: Kruskal–Wallis tests across intensities, Spearman correlations, and regressions on design intensity.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) no eye tracking, online instead of a lab; (2) pictures are cut from the published figure (about 300 × 227 px); (3) the figure does not label which picture has which design intensity, so researchers need to code intensity (for example by counting added elements) before analysis; (4) the major question is free text.",
