as sufficient content and structure that it can occupy my mind for a long period.\",\n      \"description\": \"Four-item restorative potential scale (Extent) with the paper's wording; 1 = fully disagree, 7 = fully agree; restorative rating = mean of the four items. One record per photo. Key = photo id: set letter a–e as in the paper's Figure 1 (a waterfront plaza, b rockery waterfall, c lawn, d garden path, e pond) plus position 1–4 in that figure panel (1 top-left, 2 top-right, 3 bottom-left, 4 bottom-right). The four photos of a set are Photoshop versions of one scene at four design intensities (lowest, low, moderate, high); the figure does not say which photo has which intensity, so code intensity before analysis (for example by counting added landscape elements). Paper's analysis: Kruskal–Wallis tests across the four intensities, Spearman correlations, and linear/curvilinear regressions of preference and restorativeness on design intensity.\",\n      \"rateMin\": 1,\n      \"rateMax\": 7,\n      \"minRateDescription\": \"Fully disagree\",\n      \"maxRateDescription\": \"Fully agree\",\n      \"isRequired\": true\n     },\n     {\n      \"type\": \"rating\",\n      \"name\": \"prs_fascination\",\n      \"title\": \"My attention is attracted by the scene.\",\n      \"description\": \"Four-item restorative potential scale (Fascination) with the paper's wording; 1 = fully disagree, 7 = fully agree; restorative rating = mean of the four items. One record per photo. Key = photo id: set letter a–e as in the paper's Figure 1 (a waterfront plaza, b rockery waterfall, c lawn, d garden path, e pond) plus position 1–4 in that figure panel (1 top-left, 2 top-right, 3 bottom-left, 4 bottom-right). The four photos of a set are Photoshop versions of one scene at four design intensities (lowest, low, moderate, high); the figure does not say which photo has which intensity, so code intensity before analysis (for example by counting added landscape elements). Paper's analysis: Kr